{"name":"Divine Wine","hex":"#583e3e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"divine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to a god.","Eternal, holy, or otherwise godlike."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One skilled in divinity; a theologian.","A minister of the gospel; a priest; a clergyman."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/divine"},{"term":"wine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An alcoholic beverage made by fermenting grape juice, with an ABV ranging from 5.5–16%.","An alcoholic beverage made by fermenting other substances, producing a similar ABV."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To entertain (someone) with wine.","To drink wine."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Divinity","blocks":[{"text":"Divinity refers to the quality, presence, or nature of that which is divine—a term that, before the rise of monotheism, evoked a broad and dynamic field of sacred power. In the ancient world, divinity was not limited to a single deity or abstract ideal but was recognized in multiple forms: as a radiant attribute possessed by gods, as a vital force cushioning nature, and even as a quality glimpsed in extraordinary humans, laws, or acts. The Latin divinitas and its Greek counterparts conveyed something both immanent and awe-inspiring: a presence that could be felt in thunder, justice, ecstasy, fate, or beauty."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Isaac_Oliver_Elizabeth_I_and_the_Three_Goddesses.jpg/330px-Isaac_Oliver_Elizabeth_I_and_the_Three_Goddesses.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divinity"},{"title":"Wine","blocks":[{"text":"Wine is an alcoholic drink made from fermented grape juice. It is produced and consumed in many regions around the world, in a wide variety of styles which are influenced by different varieties of grapes, growing environments, viticulture methods, and production techniques."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Red_and_white_wine_in_glass.jpg/330px-Red_and_white_wine_in_glass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wine"},{"title":"Wine red","blocks":[{"text":"The color wine, or wine red, also called bordeaux, vinous, or vinaceous, is a dark shade of red and brown. It is a representation of the typical color of red wine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c0/Red_Wine_Glass.jpg/330px-Red_Wine_Glass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wine_red"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Alexandr_Ivanov_015.jpg/500px-Alexandr_Ivanov_015.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Alexandr_Ivanov_015.jpg/500px-Alexandr_Ivanov_015.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Transfiguration by Alexandr Ivanov, 1824","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlexandr_Ivanov_015.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Hildegard_von_Bingen.jpg/500px-Hildegard_von_Bingen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Hildegard_von_Bingen.jpg/1280px-Hildegard_von_Bingen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Hildegard_von_Bingen.jpg/500px-Hildegard_von_Bingen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hildegard von Bingen receives a divine inspiration and passes it on to her scribe","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHildegard_von_Bingen.jpg"},{"srcset